From 616f614c61b4e87e0b106267ba566de552fcccd2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 06:23:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] player information: what the TV declares in its EDID, for the
 system page

A TV's EDID changes with its own settings (QMS and FreeSync on an LG, its
game mode), and the player information did not show it. Its system page
says now what the display declares, read from the display information
that the decisions of Dolby Vision use (DeclaredDisplay(), the one that
DisplayModel() names), and only when that is another one: PublishPlayerInfo()
takes it again once per display change, not at its two beats a second
(DeclaresOf(), hades.tv.*):
 - the HDR formats: HDR10 and HLG by the EOTFs of the HDR static metadata
   block, HDR10+ by its block of any application version ("HDR10+ (v0)"
   for version 0);
 - Dolby Vision: the modes of its block as CDisplayInfo reads it for the
   decisions (Standard, Low-Latency, both, or the older signalling of HDMI
   1.4b) and the version of the block; "unread" for a block that is not
   taken (DolbyVisionBlockInvalid());
 - from the HDMI Forum block (HF-VSDB or HF-SCDB) as libdisplay-info reads
   it, the most that either says (CDisplayInfo::GetHdmiForumSink()): the
   VRR range, the fastest FRL (FrlLanesAndGbps(): the numbers of the FRL
   rate of NV_OUTPUT_LINK are those of the EDID) or TMDS only with its
   fastest rate, ALLM and QMS;
 - FreeSync: AMD's vendor-specific data block (OUI 00-00-1A) is there
   (EdidDeclaresFreeSync(), new). Only that it is there: AMD publishes
   none of its fields, and the kernel reads those of version 3 only,
   which say nothing of refresh rates (drm_edid.c, amd_vsdb_v3_payload).
Without an EDID that could be read the keys stay empty, and the page says
so. The log of the display information says the HDMI Forum block and
FreeSync too, once per EDID.

Checked on the host: EdidDeclaresFreeSync() on three real EDIDs (two
monitors with AMD's block, a TV without it) and on copies of them with a
wrong checksum, another OUI, a block too short for the OUI, and on a
block made by hand: each as expected.
